  <section class="faith-intro">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <details class="faith-intro-disclosure" data-faith-intro>
        <summary class="faith-intro-summary">
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-label" data-faith-intro-label>Read introduction</span>
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faith-intro-body">
          <p class="faith-intro-prose hero-excerpt-dropcap">Amandus Polanus von Polansdorf (1561-1610) wrote the Syntagma Theologiae Christianae as a working textbook for the Reformed academy at Basel; the first folio appeared in 1609, the definitive Hanau edition in 1615. It is the first fully systematic Reformed scholastic theology, organised on the bifurcating Ramist method: every locus is divided into two, then two again, until the entire matter of dogmatics has been distributed across ten books. Wollebius, Maccovius, Gomarus and the wider Dutch Reformed tradition all leaned on it. This English edition is Stiven Peter’s translation of the SCTA diplomatic transcription of the 1615 Hanau folio, paired with a facing Latin-English verification edition and released, like the source, under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. As of this edition only Liber I (De Theologiae Principiis - on the principles of theology) is available; the remaining nine books will be added as the translation progresses.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
    
